p\" — no consistency check needed. Keeping the predicate would be misleading: it would still report a value, but no caller would or should consult it. Its test (`TestV2CheckpointFullyMigrated`) was a unit test for a predicate that no longer has a job.\n\n**Trivial wrapper — deletion was a judgment call but defensible:**\n\n- `hasFullSessionArtifacts` (the migrate-package wrapper): wrapped `v2Store.HasFullSessionArtifacts` only to add an error prefix. After deletes, only one test still used it, and that test was already speaking to the store. Inlining felt cleaner than keeping a one-line wrapper.\n\n**The one I'm least sure about:**\n\n- `V2GitStore.CleanupV1TranscriptFiles`: this is the *public store method*, not the migrate-side wrapper. Reasoning for deleting: under the new semantics, the only path that touches v2 destructively is `--force`, and that path prunes the whole checkpoint subtree via `pruneV2CheckpointForForce` — which removes legacy `full.jsonl`/`content_hash.txt` along with everything else. So the targeted cleanup is redundant for every current code path. Reasoning for keeping: it's tested store-level functionality that could plausibly come back as part of an `entire migrate --cleanup-legacy` command someday.\n\nIf you'd rather I restore `V2GitStore.CleanupV1TranscriptFiles` (and its 3 tests) as a \"we may want this later\" stash, say the word and I'll bring it back. Everything else I'd argue was downstream of the behavior change — leaving it would have left a working alternate implementation of the path you just rejected."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":902,"cache_read_input_tokens":275097,"output_tokens":2645,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":902,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":6,"output_tokens":2645,"cache_