omes huge and the `.git/packed-refs` grows. The whole point of generations as batches goes away.\n\n2. **Reverse write order**: write the raw transcript to `/full/current` first, then write `/main`. Periodically archive `/full/current` → `/full/<N>` once it crosses the threshold (the runtime path's existing rotation logic does this). Now `existing != nil` on `/main` *implies* the raw transcript already landed on some `/full/*` ref. Cost: a git commit on `/full/current` per checkpoint instead of one per batch — ~50× more commit objects on that ref during migration. Still O(checkpoints), but distributed differently.\n\n3. **Add an explicit `--resume` flag** that does what the old slow path did, but only when the user opts in: detect checkpoints with `/main` but missing `/full`, queue them for repacking, leave fully-migrated ones alone. No-flag invocation stays a hard skip; the silent-magic path becomes opt-in.\n\n   This is closest in spirit to what you said today: rerun-with-no-flag is no-op, full stop. Recovery is explicit. `--force` redoes everything; `--resume` redoes only the half-finished ones.\n\nMy read: option 3 is the right shape — it preserves the rule you set and gives users a non-sledgehammer recovery for the Ctrl+C case. Option 2 is also reasonable and avoids needing a new flag at all (resume is automatic because `/main` and `/full` stay in sync). Option 1 is correct but the ref-count explosion is ugly.\n\nWant me to implement one of these, or document the limitation and leave `--force` as the only recovery for now?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1521,"cache_read_input_tokens":312924,"output_tokens":5245,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1521,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":6,"output_tokens":5245,"cache_read_inp